---
import "../styles/global.css";

const basePath = import.meta.env.BASE_URL.replace(/\/?$/, "/");
const scoutExample = JSON.stringify({
  war_summary: "FIELD DESK · Day 113 — Oarbreaker remains the busiest front...",
  selected_regions: ["OarbreakerHex", "ClahstraHex", "MooringCountyHex"],
}, null, 2);
const forecastExample = JSON.stringify({
  predictions: [{
    rank: 1,
    base_id: "OarbreakerHex:example",
    outcome: "CAPTURED_BY_COLONIALS",
    eta_utc: "2026-08-24T08:15:00Z",
    confidence: 0.62,
    sigma_minutes: 45,
    evidence: [{ metric_id: "region.OarbreakerHex.totalEnlistments.rate_1h_per_hour", relevance: 8 }],
  }],
}, null, 2);
const adviceExample = JSON.stringify({
  strategic_advice: {
    colonial_reinforce: {
      base_id: "OarbreakerHex:example",
      reason: "Recent pressure and ownership changes make this a fragile point.",
      evidence: [{ metric_id: "region.OarbreakerHex.wardenCasualties.rate_1h_per_hour", relevance: 7 }],
    },
  },
}, null, 2);
---

<!doctype html>
<html lang="en">
  <head>
    <meta charset="UTF-8" />
    <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width" />
    <meta name="description" content="A beginner-friendly walkthrough of the Foxhole Forecast prompt and evidence flow." />
    <title>Prompt Flow · Foxhole Forecast</title>
  </head>
  <body>
    <header class="method-masthead">
      <div class="masthead__eyebrow">UNDER THE HOOD · SAME DATA, FROZEN CUTOFF, NO HINDSIGHT</div>
      <div class="method-hero page-width">
        <div>
          <p class="kicker">Inside one round</p>
          <h1>How a forecast<br /><span>gets made</span></h1>
          <p class="sublede">The whole pipeline in plain view: what the model saw, what it wrote, what it bet on, and how the game later proved it right or wrong.</p>
        </div>
        <a class="method-back" href={basePath}>← Return to dashboard</a>
      </div>
    </header>

    <nav class="section-nav" aria-label="Prompt flow navigation">
      <div class="page-width">
        <a href={basePath}>Live dashboard</a>
        <a href={`${basePath}predictions/`}>Round history</a>
        <a href={`${basePath}history/`}>Summary archive</a>
      </div>
    </nav>

    <main class="prompt-flow-page page-width">
      <section class="method-intro prompt-flow-intro">
        <p class="kicker">The basic idea</p>
        <p>No model sees a live feed. Each one gets a frozen snapshot, writes a short dispatch, picks the regions worth watching, then commits to eight timed predictions — plus four friendly recommendations that we deliberately don’t score.</p>
        <aside class="method-callout"><strong>Why unscored advice?</strong><span>The recommendations (who should reinforce or attack what) are answered into the void: no player ever saw them, so nothing that happens later can prove a recommendation <em>caused</em> anything. We keep them for colour and comparison — the eight predictions carry all the scoring.</span></aside>
      </section>

      <section class="flow-section" aria-labelledby="flow-title">
        <div class="scoring-section__heading"><p class="kicker">01–07</p><h2 id="flow-title">One round, step by step</h2></div>
        <div class="flow-timeline">
          <article class="flow-step"><span>01</span><div><h3>The snapshot</h3><p>At a shared UTC moment, the model sees the whole public map: who owns each base, recent captures, casualty pace, and enlistment activity.</p><small>Every model in the round gets the identical snapshot.</small></div></article>
          <article class="flow-step"><span>02</span><div><h3>Dispatch and picks</h3><p>First it writes a short newspaper-style summary of the war, then names up to six regions whose detailed history it wants to inspect.</p><small>The summary is archived as the model’s briefing.</small></div></article>
          <article class="flow-step"><span>03</span><div><h3>A closer look</h3><p>It receives deeper, cutoff-safe history for exactly those regions. No facts from after the cutoff can leak in — the data was frozen beforehand.</p><small>Which regions a model asks for is itself something we can inspect.</small></div></article>
          <article class="flow-step"><span>04</span><div><h3>Eight timed calls</h3><p>Now it commits: eight different bases, what will happen to each, an exact UTC time, a confidence, a timing spread, and the evidence it relied on.</p><small>Ranks 1–4 target the next six hours; ranks 5–8 target the following day.</small></div></article>
          <article class="flow-step"><span>05</span><div><h3>Four friendly tips</h3><p>It also answers four neutral prompts — where each faction should reinforce or attack. Advice only; these are never scored.</p><small>Each tip carries its own reasoning and evidence.</small></div></article>
          <article class="flow-step"><span>06</span><div><h3>Locked in</h3><p>Predictions, recommendations, cutoff, evidence, and the raw model response are all stored. Nothing can be edited later to look smarter.</p><small>That’s what makes it a forecast instead of hindsight.</small></div></article>
          <article class="flow-step"><span>07</span><div><h3>Reality votes</h3><p>We keep polling the official API every fifteen minutes. Ownership changes are observed in intervals, and the eight calls are scored against what actually happened.</p><small>The unscored tips can be compared descriptively with later activity — but never treated as cause and effect.</small></div></article>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section class="prompt-example-section" aria-labelledby="examples-title">
        <div class="scoring-section__heading"><p class="kicker">Illustrative, trimmed examples</p><h2 id="examples-title">What the model actually returns</h2></div>
        <p class="method-caption">Below is the shape of the exchange, with fictional values. The forecast response actually contains both the predictions and the advice blocks shown separately here. Trimmed for readability — the full prompt files are linked at the bottom.</p>
        <div class="prompt-example-grid">
          <details class="prompt-example" open>
            <summary>Scout reply</summary>
            <pre><code>{scoutExample}</code></pre>
          </details>
          <details class="prompt-example">
            <summary>Forecast reply</summary>
            <pre><code>{forecastExample}</code></pre>
          </details>
          <details class="prompt-example">
            <summary>Friendly tips</summary>
            <pre><code>{adviceExample}</code></pre>
          </details>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section class="prompt-access-section" aria-labelledby="prompt-access-title">
        <div class="scoring-section__heading"><p class="kicker">Full source text</p><h2 id="prompt-access-title">Read the exact prompts</h2></div>
        <p>The page keeps things readable; the repository keeps the truth. The complete prompt text — including output rules and validation constraints — is public.</p>
        <div class="prompt-links"><a href="https://github.com/fh-eval/foxhole-forecast/blob/main/prompts/scout.md" rel="noreferrer">Open full scout prompt ↗</a><a href="https://github.com/fh-eval/foxhole-forecast/blob/main/prompts/forecast.md" rel="noreferrer">Open full forecast prompt ↗</a><a href="https://github.com/fh-eval/foxhole-forecast/tree/main/prompts" rel="noreferrer">Browse all prompt versions ↗</a></div>
        <p class="method-caption">The prompts ask only for conclusions supported by the snapshot. No model gets player communications, hidden inventories, or an in-game agent.</p>
      </section>
    </main>

    <footer><div class="page-width"><strong>FOXHOLE FORECAST</strong><a href={basePath}>Live dashboard</a></div></footer>
  </body>
</html>
